course: dictionary
lesson: 9259
title: Sourdough: one word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The clues around a word tell you which meaning the writer means. «Sourdough uses wild yeast and bacteria; the bacteria make acids that give the sour taste» is about bread, because yeast, bacteria and sour taste all belong to baking.
explain: Ask yourself what the word is doing in the sentence. In «a sourdough loaf», sourdough is a kind of bread. In «feed the starter», the starter is the mixture you keep alive with flour and water, and sourdough is the bread it helps to make.
explain: The third meaning is old and from America. A sourdough was an old-timer in Alaska or the Yukon, usually a gold prospector. If the sentence talks about gold, mining or the far north, that is the clue to choose this meaning instead of bread.
fact: Sourdough is bread made with a starter instead of packet yeast.
fact: A sourdough starter is a bubbly mix of flour and water, full of wild yeast and bacteria, which is kept alive by feeding it.
fact: Wild yeast makes carbon dioxide gas, which makes the bread rise.
fact: Bacteria make acid, which gives sourdough its slightly sour taste.
fact: (US, old) A sourdough was an old-timer in Alaska or the Yukon, especially a gold prospector.
exercise: «Many families bake a sourdough loaf at home.» What is a sourdough loaf? | A kind of gold mine | A type of Alaskan boat | A bubbly drink for a party | * A loaf of bread made with a starter
exercise: «The bacteria make acids that give the sour taste.» What gives sourdough its sour taste? | * Acid made by bacteria | Packet yeast | Salt from the sea | Carbon dioxide gas
exercise: «Feed the starter with flour and water.» What does feed the starter mean? | * Add flour and water to keep the starter alive | Eat the starter with a spoon | Freeze the starter for ever | Throw the starter away
test: What makes sourdough bread rise? | Salt | Cold water | Acid from bacteria | * Carbon dioxide gas from wild yeast
test: Read this: «The old sourdough walked into town with a bag of gold dust.» Which meaning fits? | A sour taste | A sourdough starter in a jar | * An old gold prospector from Alaska or the Yukon | Bread made with a starter
exam: «The sourdough hauled his gold up the frozen river, far from any bakery.» What does sourdough mean here? | A starter kept alive with flour and water | A sour sort of acid | * An old gold prospector, an old-timer from the far north | Bread made with wild yeast
